epublican lean is the strongest available evidence, since no general-election polls or expert ratings exist yet; early primary activity shows Republicans consolidating around Vice President Vance while Democrats remain unsettled, giving little basis to move the number in either direction.", "watch": "The national environment in 2028, the eventual nominees, and any serious Democratic investment in the state could shift the race; the first credible general-election polls will be the key signal.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Texas remains a Republican-leaning state in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of about six points toward the GOP. The 2028 race is an open contest: the incumbent president is term-limited, and neither party has a nominee. Early attention on the Republican side centers on Vice President JD Vance, who has won CPAC straw polls and led early primary surveys, while the Democratic field remains unsettled, with primary polls showing a tight race.\n\nNo general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so the forecast rests on the state's fundamental lean. That points to a Republican advantage of roughly six points, with wide uncertainty given the early date and the absence of polling. Texas has backed the Republican nominee in every recent presidential election, but an open field and a national environment that could shift leave room for movement in either direction.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2466, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2898, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2886, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2879, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2903, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem":